Thus missing focus restoration.

In show() method, they do:
this._element.focus()
this._element.setAttribute('aria-expanded', true)

Thus they focus toggle on show. On hide, they should return focus to toggle (if it had focus before opening). Usually pattern: when closing a menu via Escape, focus returns to the button that opened it.

Thus we need to modify hide() or _completeHide() to focus the toggle after hiding, if appropriate.

But we must consider that sometimes the toggle might not have focus originally (e.g., user navigated into menu via keyboard and then pressed Escape). According to ARIA authoring practices, when a menu button (menubar) opens a menu, focus moves to first item. When Escape closes menu, focus returns to the button.

Thus we should focus the toggle after hiding.

Implementation: In _completeHide, after removing classes and setting aria-expanded false, we can call this._element.focus().

However, we need to ensure we don't break cases where hide() is called programmatically (e.g., via clicking outside). But focusing toggle may still be appropriate.

